# Forms and API

## Quote form goal

The quote form should collect enough information for a wholesale response without creating unnecessary friction.

Recommended fields:
- name
- company
- email
- phone
- business type
- products of interest
- quantity/requirement
- delivery location
- message

Only require fields that are essential for the workflow.

## Submission flow

Preferred flow:
Visitor -> form -> Astro server endpoint -> validation -> spam checks -> email provider -> wholesale desk

Use Resend by default unless the project explicitly chooses Postmark.

## Validation

Validate on the server even if the client validates.

Validate:
- required fields
- email format
- string lengths
- reasonable payload sizes
- allowed values for select fields

Trim and normalize strings before processing.

Never trust client-provided content.

## Security

Never expose:
- email API keys
- Sanity private tokens
- database credentials
- environment secrets

Use environment variables.

Return generic errors to the client.
Do not leak stack traces or internal service errors.

## Anti-spam

Add proportionate protection such as:
- honeypot field
- timing checks where appropriate
- server-side rate limiting if available
- CAPTCHA only when spam volume warrants it

Do not make the form hostile to legitimate B2B users.

## Success state

After success, make the outcome clear.
Example:
"Thanks. Your enquiry has been received. Our wholesale team will get back to you shortly."

Avoid showing a fake success message before the server confirms delivery.

## Future database

Do not add Postgres merely because it may be useful later.

When enquiry search, auditability, portal accounts, or workflows genuinely require persistence, add Neon/Postgres behind the same server boundary.

The public pages should not become dependent on the future portal architecture.
